course: world
lesson: 183
title: Sounds right, but is wrong
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: These answers jump out at you, sound perfect, and are wrong. A bat and a ball cost £1.10 and the bat costs £1 more: "The ball is 10p!" says everyone. Check it: a £1.10 bat plus a 10p ball is £1.20. The ball is 5p and the bat £1.05.
explain: The habit that saves you takes ten seconds: put your answer back into the story and see whether it fits.
explain: Many traps come from thinking in straight lines: things that double don't grow evenly (lily pads), handshakes grow faster than the number of kids, and a price that goes up by half and then down by half doesn't come back.
explain: Others hide a fence-post (6 strikes of a clock have only 5 gaps between them) or a whole hiding inside a part (a brick that weighs 1 kg and half a brick weighs 2 kg, not 1 and a half).
explain: Freddie's sister: "Check it, bro. Your answer's 10p too expensive." Laugh at the answer, check it, try again.
fact: If a bat and a ball cost £1.10 together and the bat costs £1 more than the ball, the ball costs 5p and the bat £1.05.
fact: Lily pads that double every day and fill the pond on day 48 covered half of it on day 47.
fact: If 5 children make 5 paper planes in 5 minutes, each child makes one plane in 5 minutes, so 100 children make 100 planes in 5 minutes.
fact: A clock striking 6 times has 5 gaps between the strikes; if that takes 5 seconds, each gap is 1 second, so 12 strikes (11 gaps) take 11 seconds.
fact: If a brick weighs 1 kg and half a brick, then the 1 kg must be the other half, so the whole brick weighs 2 kg, not 1 and a half.
fact: A £10 toy that goes up by half costs £15; taking half off £15 leaves £7.50, not £10.
fact: Balls of a similar shape fall at almost the same speed whatever their weight; air only slows light, fluffy things like feathers a lot.
fact: On the Moon, where there is no air, an astronaut dropped a hammer and a feather in 1971 and they landed at the same moment.
fact: Galileo studied falling things about 400 years ago and showed that weight matters far less than people thought.
fact: A 1-in-6 chance of a six is a chance on each roll, not a promise: you can roll 6 times and get no six at all, and that happens about 1 time in 3.
fact: The gap between two people's ages stays the same: when a 5-year-old reaches 10, his brother who is 5 years older is 15.
fact: With 8 children each shaking hands once with every other child, each shakes 7 hands, and 8 × 7 = 56 counts every handshake twice, so there are 28; with 4 children there are 6.
fact: Words people use: "fam" (London, young people) means mates; "can or not?" (Singapore) means "is it possible?"; "lah" (Singapore) is added to make a sentence sound friendly; "mate" means friend.
exercise: A bat and a ball cost £1.10 together. The bat costs £1 more than the ball. How much is the ball? | 5p | 10p | £1 | 55p
exercise: If the ball cost 10p, what would the bat and ball cost together? | £1.20 | £1.10 | £1.00 | £1.11
exercise: Lily pads on a pond double every day, and on day 48 the pond is full. When was it half full? | Day 47 | Day 24 | Day 46 | Day 12
exercise: If 5 kids make 5 paper planes in 5 minutes, how long do 100 kids need to make 100 paper planes? | 5 minutes | 100 minutes | 20 minutes | 500 minutes
exercise: A clock strikes 6 times in 5 seconds at 6 o'clock. How long does it take to strike 12 times at noon? | 11 seconds | 10 seconds | 12 seconds | 6 seconds
exercise: How many gaps are there between 6 strikes of a clock? | 5 | 6 | 7 | 12
exercise: A brick weighs 1 kg and half a brick. How much does the whole brick weigh? | 2 kg | 1 and a half kg | 2 and a half kg | 3 and a half kg
exercise: In the brick puzzle, what is the 1 kg really? | The other half | The whole brick | A quarter of the brick | Two whole bricks stuck together
exercise: A toy costs £10. The price goes up by half, then down by half. What does it cost now? | £7.50 | £10 | £5 | £12.50
exercise: Max drops a big ball and a small ball from the top of the slide at the same moment. What happens? | They land at almost the same time | The big ball lands well before the small one | The small ball lands well before the big one | The big ball drifts down slowly
exercise: What happened when an astronaut dropped a hammer and a feather on the Moon? | They landed together | The hammer landed first | The feather landed first | The feather floated away
exercise: Mia: "There's a 1 in 6 chance of a six, so if I roll 6 times I'll definitely get one." What is true? | It's a chance on each roll, not a promise; she may get no six | A six is certain within 6 rolls | After 5 misses the sixth roll must be a six | Each roll makes the next six less likely
exercise: Jamal is 10 and his little brother is 5. How old will Jamal be when his brother is 10? | 15 | 20 | 10 | 25
exercise: 4 kids at a party each shake hands with every other kid once: 6 handshakes. How many handshakes for 8 kids? | 28 | 12 | 16 | 56
exercise: Why isn't 8 kids just double the handshakes of 4 kids? | Each kid also has more people to shake hands with | Some of the kids refuse to shake hands | Each handshake is counted once per hand | Only half of the kids shake hands
exercise: What is the best habit for answers that "sound right"? | Put your answer back into the story and check it | Go with the first number that pops into your head | Pick the answer with the roundest number | Double all the numbers in the question
test: Freddie's sister: "Check it, bro, your answer's 10p too expensive." A pencil and a rubber cost £1.20 together, and the pencil costs £1 more than the rubber. How much is the rubber? | 10p | 20p | £1 | 5p
test: Ruby: "Day 24, easy!" Teacher: "On day 24 it was tiny, like a freckle." If a doubling pond fills on day 30, when was it a quarter full? | Day 28 | Day 15 | Day 29 | Day 7
test: Toby: "100 minutes, fam!" If 3 bakers each bake one cake in 3 hours, how long do 6 bakers take to bake 6 cakes, one each? | 3 hours | 6 hours | 1 and a half hours | 12 hours
test: Ava's dad: "Twelve strikes, eleven gaps, eleven seconds." A clock strikes 3 times in 2 seconds. How long does it take to strike 9 times? | 8 seconds | 6 seconds | 9 seconds | 4 and a half seconds
test: Mum: "One and a half? Put it back in the story, Pete. Does it fit?" A bag of flour weighs 2 kg and half a bag. How much does the whole bag weigh? | 4 kg altogether | 3 kg | 2 kg and a bit | 2 kg
test: The shop assistant: "Up to £15, then half off, £7.50. Bargain, mate." What if the £10 toy went down by half first, then up by half? | £7.50 | £10 | £5 | £15
test: Max: "Heavy stuff falls faster, everyone knows." His friend: "Galileo already did this, about 400 years ago." What happens if you drop a feather and a ball in the classroom? | The ball lands first, because air slows the feather a lot | They land at exactly the same moment | The feather lands first | The feather falls upwards
test: Mia's brother: "Definitely, she said. Rolled six times, no six." What is the real lesson? | 1 in 6 is a chance each roll, not a promise in 6 rolls | The dice must be broken | A six must come by the sixth roll | Mia should have rolled faster
test: Jamal's mum: "You don't get extra birthdays for being the oldest, love." When Jamal is 30, how old will his brother be? | 25 | 15 | 20 | 35
test: Ellie: "My hand hurts, this party is a handshake factory." How many handshakes are there with 5 kids, each shaking every other hand once? | 10 | 8 | 20 | 6
test: A cousin in Singapore: "Ball costs 10 cents lah, can or not?" A bat and ball cost $1.10 and the bat costs $1 more. What is the answer? | Cannot; the ball costs 5 cents | Can; the ball costs 10 cents | Cannot; the ball costs 1 cent | Can; the bat costs exactly $1
test: In a cartoon a dragon's gold pile doubles every day and fills the cave on day 100. The dragon says, "So it was half full on day 50!" Put the cartoon right. | It was half full on day 99 | It was half full on day 50 | It was half full on day 75 | It was half full on day 98
exam: It is given that a bat and a ball together cost one pound and ten pence and that the bat costs one pound more than the ball. Ascertain the price of the ball. | Five pence | Ten pence | One pound | Fifty-five pence
exam: A clock strikes six times in five seconds, the interval between successive strikes being constant. Determine the time required for twelve strikes. | Eleven seconds | Ten seconds | Twelve seconds | Six seconds
exam: An article priced at ten pounds is increased in price by one half and is subsequently reduced by one half. Compute the final price. | Seven pounds and fifty pence | Ten pounds | Five pounds | Twelve pounds and fifty pence
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a hammer and a feather released simultaneously upon the surface of the Moon. | Both reach the surface at the same moment | The hammer reaches the surface first | The feather reaches the surface first | The feather does not descend at all
